bpftrace
High-level tracing language for Linux eBPF

github: https://github.com/bpftrace/bpftrace
license: Apache 2.0
license url: https://github.com/bpftrace/bpftrace/blob/master/LICENSE

The selected release is defined in `projects/versions.mk`.

Android tracefs fallback:
- `build.mk` applies `tracefs_fallback_$(BPFTRACE_COMMIT).patch`, currently
  `tracefs_fallback_v0.27.1.patch`, against the selected upstream release tag.
  Patch contents participate in the source signature, including for the
  standalone static build. A version update requires a matching patch.
- If libbpf returns EPERM or ENOENT for a single kprobe/kretprobe, the fallback
  creates a tracefs event and attaches its BPF program on each online CPU.
  Event names include a PID and attachment counter. The attachment object owns
  the perf FDs and closes them before removing the event, also on failure.
- Exact kprobes may proceed when the kernel's traceable-function list is empty;
  the kernel still validates the attachment. This does not enable wildcard
  expansion without a function list, or bypass kernel permission checks.
- Run `make check-bpftrace-patches` from the repository root to fetch the selected
  bpftrace release and validate it. CI runs the same target before release builds.
  It applies both patches to pristine tagged files in a
  temporary directory and fault-tests the actual patched attachment class with
  mocked kernel/libbpf calls. It does not test real kernel attachment.

Building:
- we need to tell cmake to use flex version that matches the lib and
  headers we have for Android. We set FLEX_EXECUTABLE to point to
  host executable built from the same sources as we use for Android.
- bpftrace links against stdc++fs which is not available in NDK as a standalone
  library. We provide an empty stub for linking purposes.
